# EaseWaybill Legal Section

How the `/legal` pages work, where things live, and how to add or edit a
policy. This file lives at the root of the legal content system — put it in
`content/legal/README.md` or `docs/README.md`, whichever your repo convention
prefers.

## How it works

Each legal document is a markdown file with frontmatter, stored in
`content/legal/`. A single component (`LegalPageShell`) renders every
document the same way; each route in `app/legal/<slug>/page.tsx` just fetches
its own document by slug and hands it to that component.

```
content/legal/<slug>.md   →  lib/legal.ts (getLegalDoc)  →  app/legal/<slug>/page.tsx  →  LegalPageShell
```

Nothing about the visual design lives in the markdown — that's all in
`LegalPageShell.tsx`. The markdown only holds title, dates, summary, and body
text, so legal copy can be edited without touching any component code.

## Folder structure

```
content/legal/          → the actual policy text (markdown + frontmatter)
app/legal/               → one route folder per policy, plus the hub page
components/legal/        → shared rendering: page shell, footer links, cookie banner
lib/legal.ts              → reads/parses the markdown files
docs/legal-launch-notes.md → nav structure, footer copy, banner copy, verification notice
internal-docs/            → regulatory gap-analysis + review checklist (NOT published)
```

## Current documents

| Slug | File | Covers |
|---|---|---|
| `privacy-policy` | `privacy-policy.md` | Data collected, why, how it's used, user rights |
| `refund-policy` | `refund-policy.md` | When refunds apply, escrow-release implications |
| `terms-and-conditions` | `terms-and-conditions.md` | Platform rules, escrow process, AI-assisted disputes |
| `cookie-policy` | `cookie-policy.md` | Cookie categories, consent, banner text |
| `accessibility-statement` | `accessibility-statement.md` | Accessibility commitments, how to report issues |
| `copyright-ip-policy` | `copyright-ip-policy.md` | Ownership, user-content licence, takedown process |
| `tracking-analytics` | `tracking-analytics.md` | Technical/usage data collected and why |
| `fake-reviews-policy` | `fake-reviews-policy.md` | Rules against fake/manipulated reviews |
| `third-party-services` | `third-party-services.md` | Paystack and other integrated services |
| `business-details` | `business-details.md` | Registered entity, contact details |

## Adding a new policy

1. Add `content/legal/<new-slug>.md` with frontmatter:
   ```markdown
   ---
   title: "Your Policy Title"
   summary: "One-line description shown on the hub page."
   effectiveDate: "YYYY-MM-DD"
   lastUpdated: "YYYY-MM-DD"
   ---

   Body content here...
   ```
2. Add `app/legal/<new-slug>/page.tsx` following the pattern used by the
   existing pages (fetch the doc by slug, pass it to `LegalPageShell`, set
   `metadata.title`/`description`).
3. Add the new link to `components/legal/LegalFooterLinks.tsx` if it belongs
   in the footer, or leave it hub-page-only if it's lower priority.
4. The hub page (`app/legal/page.tsx`) picks up every document in
   `content/legal/` automatically — no change needed there.

## Editing an existing policy

Edit the markdown file directly. Update `lastUpdated` in the frontmatter
whenever the body text changes materially — that date is what renders next
to the title on both the hub page and the document page.

## Before publishing anything

Every document currently in `content/legal/` contains bracketed placeholders
(`[INSERT ...]`) for business details, dates, and operational specifics (fee
schedule, dispute windows, retention periods) that have not been confirmed
yet. **Do not replace a placeholder with an invented value** — leave it until
the real figure is confirmed.

More importantly: these documents are first drafts. They have not been
reviewed by a Nigerian lawyer. See `internal-docs/legal-review-checklist.md`
for the full list of what needs legal and operational confirmation —
including, as the highest-priority open item, whether EaseWaybill's
escrow/fund-holding model requires any separate CBN licensing beyond routing
payments through Paystack. Nothing in `content/legal/` should be treated as
final or go live until that review is complete.